('h3', '5.3  Code Snippets with Explanation'),
('h5', '5.3.1  Window-level conformal calibration (guard.py)'),
('p', "The guard is calibrated on sliding windows. For every window length L used by the service, the family-wise score of every historical window in the calibration period is computed, and the empirical quantile at level 1 minus alpha (alpha = 0.01) is stored. A value-level bound would give a much higher window-level false-alarm rate (about 24 percent was measured with a per-value alpha before this correction), which is why the quantile is taken over the maximum score of a window."),
('code', 'inference-api/guard.py', 'def calibrate_windows', 24, 'Listing 5.1  Family-wise calibration of the envelope'),
('h5', '5.3.2  The validity score (guard.py)'),
('p', "score() returns a single number that is at most 1 for a valid forecast. It normalises the envelope score and, in envelope+inv mode, the invariant score by the calibrated window quantiles and returns the larger of the two. A NaN output gives a non-finite score and is therefore always rejected."),
('code', 'inference-api/guard.py', 'def score', 11, 'Listing 5.2  Window-level validity score'),
('h5', '5.3.3  Golden-output integrity check (app.py)'),
('p', "At start-up the service stores a 24-step forecast for each model. golden_dev() returns the largest deviation of a later forecast from it, in units of the standard deviation of the history of each variable, and infinity if any value is not finite. Because the models are deterministic the healthy deviation is zero; the tolerance 0.002 only absorbs floating-point noise. The check catches the subtle bit-flip fault that stays inside the envelope."),
('code', 'inference-api/app.py', 'def golden_dev', 14, 'Listing 5.3  Golden-output deviation'),
('h5', '5.3.4  Deadline- and load-aware tier call (app.py)'),
('p', "timed_run() is the only place where a model is called when DEADLINE_MS is set. It first tries to take a slot in the tier's bounded semaphore without waiting; if the queue is full the tier is shed with reason load. It then checks the remaining budget, submits the call to a worker pool and waits for at most the remaining time; a timeout sheds the tier with reason timeout. The semaphore is released by the worker itself, so a stuck call keeps occupying its slot and cannot be piled up by later requests."),
('code', 'inference-api/app.py', 'def timed_run', 27, 'Listing 5.4  Deadline- and load-aware call'),
('h5', '5.3.5  Tier fall-through with circuit breaker (app.py)'),
('p', "serve() walks the tier chain. A tier that is not ready or was marked suspect by the canary is skipped; a tier whose output fails the envelope, invariant or golden check is marked suspect (circuit breaker) and the next tier is tried; the climatology tier and the cache are the last resort. The first tier whose output passes every check answers and its forecast is cached."),
('code', 'inference-api/app.py', '    for tier in tier_chain(model):', 30, 'Listing 5.5  Tier chain inside serve()'),
('h5', '5.3.6  Operator decision and reconcile loop (forecast_operator.py)'),
('p', "The operator reads /health/semantic every five seconds, derives a signature (ok, unreachable, or invalid with the list of invalid models) and counts consecutive bad checks. When the threshold is reached and the cool-down has passed, choose() returns the action: the rules policy always restarts; the bandit policy looks the signature up in the policy table and picks the action with the lowest expected cost. Restart is performed by patching the pod template annotation, which triggers a rolling update."),
('code', 'operator/forecast_operator.py', 'def signature', 22, 'Listing 5.6  Signature and policy choice'),
('h5', '5.3.7  Cost model of the digital twin (twin_bandit.py)'),
('p', "The first version of the cost ignored the fact that a tier switch leaves the service on a fallback tier forever, so tier_switch looked cheapest and the bandit never restarted (restored only 5 of 20 trials). The corrected cost adds W_Q seconds for every second spent below full quality, with a 240-second horizon when full quality never returns."),
('code', 'analysis/twin_bandit.py', "        full = HORIZON if", 3, 'Listing 5.7  Quality term added to the cost'),

('h5', '5.3.8  Container image of the inference service (Dockerfile)'),
('p', "The inference image starts gunicorn with one worker and sixteen threads (gthread). One worker keeps a single copy of the models in memory (a TensorFlow process needs about 1 GiB), and the thread count was raised from four to sixteen after a burst experiment showed that four threads were exhausted by queued requests, which had been mistaken for a CPU limit. The timeout of 120 seconds only protects against a stuck worker; request-level deadlines are handled in the application."),
('code', 'inference-api/Dockerfile', 'FROM', 12, 'Listing 5.8  Dockerfile of the inference service'),
('h5', '5.3.9  Unit test of the guard (test_guard.py)'),
('p', "The unit tests construct a synthetic monthly data set with a seasonal pattern and check the three basic behaviours of the guard: a NaN forecast and a grossly scaled forecast are rejected, and the climatology forecast is accepted. They run in under a second and are part of the validation suite."),
('code', 'inference-api/tests/test_guard.py', 'def test_nan_and_outlier_rejected_clean_passes', 8, 'Listing 5.9  Guard unit test'),
('h5', '5.3.10  Event-driven scaling object (KEDA)'),
('p', "The KEDA object scales the inference deployment between one and three replicas using a Prometheus query on the request rate. It was used in the scaling extra; on a single node it did not improve the burst outcome, which is reported as a limitation."),
('code', 'k8s/keda-scaledobject.yaml', 'apiVersion', 16, 'Listing 5.10  KEDA ScaledObject'),
